WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.461 --> 00:00:03.044
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:06.830
- So Joe, I don't know if you know there's

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.910
I don't know, somewhere
around 7, 8, 9, 10 thousand

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.070
astrophysicists in the world,

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:15.600
and about, what are we up
to, 7, 8 billion people.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.360
If you divide those two numbers,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.810
an astrophysicist is one
in million in the world.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.643
- Whoa.

00:00:21.643 --> 00:00:23.750
- So if you're ever in
the same room with one,

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.380
that's when you need to
ask all the questions.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.510
'Cause you just never know
the next chance that's coming.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.240
So do you have any questions that

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.130
you've been harboring your whole life

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.680
about the universe that
you can ask right now?

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.933
- Yes, I do.
- What's that?

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:38.520
- Well I was thinking just before that,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.580
I wonder if it's the
same amount as comedians.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.150
Like professional comedians.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.940
I bet it's really similar.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.230
- Okay.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:51.230
However, comedians as a practice

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.350
are always in front of many people.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.970
- Yes.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:59.260
- Being a comedian, you are
in front of tons of people.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.642
Being a scientist, we're
never in front of anybody.

00:01:01.642 --> 00:01:02.475
(chuckles)

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:04.700
So access to comedians
I think is much higher

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.800
than access to scientists,
to astrophysicists.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.350
But those numbers could be the same.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.940
- I bet they're pretty close.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.123
So here's my question.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.850
The concept of the multiverse,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.910
the concept of alternative universes

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:25.910
with alternative laws of physics

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.720
is incredibly puzzling to me.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.280
Now when you think about the idea

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.440
that this could be reality,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.850
that there could be other universes

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.320
with different laws of physics,

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.390
there could be an infinite number

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.733
of these different laws of physics.

00:01:45.711 --> 00:01:46.736
How does that work?

00:01:46.736 --> 00:01:47.569
(Neil chuckles)

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:49.580
Okay, that's an excellent question.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.160
We think, when I say we, just
the scientific community,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.460
physicists who concern themselves

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.640
with the very early universe, cosmologist,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.563
that the laws of physics
as we experience them,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.433
are set in the very earliest
stages of the universe.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.480
And quantum fluctuations, in everything,

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.570
would be responsible for another universe

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:23.540
having slightly different
laws of physics than ours.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.940
Because the quantum fluctuations

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.670
will take it in a slightly different

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.410
law of physics direction
than our universe.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.170
And this would just keep going.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.650
Every universe that's born.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.700
Even if it started out sort of the same

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:41.890
in the very first instant,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.850
a later instant with other
laws of physics manifest

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.393
could be slightly different.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.390
And with the multiverse
where there could be

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.140
pockets of the universe that are expanding

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.530
with no knowledge of any
other pockets of the universe.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:00.070
These are essentially,
independent universes

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:03.850
from one another, and
never the twain will meet.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.150
Imagine you're a ship at sea
and you look to the horizon,

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.610
and that's your whole universe
there, to the horizon.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.250
There's another ship that
has it's own horizon,

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.820
and you declare this is the universe

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:16.300
and these ships don't
even see each other, okay?

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.980
You'll only see each other if somehow,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.490
your two horizons can overlap.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.520
And we don't know how to
do that in our universe

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.273
'cause they're noncausal.

00:03:27.741 --> 00:03:29.570
You'd have to find some way to tunnel

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.820
from one universe to the
other in order to access that.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.890
But that could be very dangerous

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.020
because if the laws of
physics are different

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.610
than the ones you evolved on,

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:43.340
then you could just
dissolve into a pile of goo

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.640
because the charge on the
electron is different,

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.893
and all of your biochemistry would change.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.850
So the difference in laws of physics

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.200
comes about because of quantum physics

00:03:59.509 --> 00:04:00.342
and, I'm gonna call it random,

00:04:00.342 --> 00:04:03.680
it's random but predictable,

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.960
so in other words, you
can have a variation

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.130
in the properties of something,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.790
and because of the quantum fluctuations

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:11.840
that exist within it.

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.220
And when that's happening
in the early universe,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.173
you are quantum fluctuating
the laws of physics themselves.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.240
And that's how you get one universe

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.630
with a slightly different laws
of physics than the other.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.810
There could be a universe where,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.140
the laws of physics there

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.290
will never allow matter to coalesce.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.270
You'll never get stars.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.820
That would be a lifeless universe.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:38.610
As far as we can think.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.640
There could be another universe
where you can make stars,

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.340
but you don't make heavy elements.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:44.710
That would be a universe with stars

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.610
and beautiful night skies as we have now,

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:51.533
but nothing that we know and love.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.483
No planets, no life.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.120
Because our ingredients come

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.730
from the centers of stars that explode.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.190
The manufacturers, the
whole periodic table

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:02.640
of elements, where does that come from?

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.540
Exploding stars, okay?

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.810
So you can imagine other universes

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:10.400
that are not our universe.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.820
There could be universes
more cooler than ours.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.510
Universes where they form life

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.163
way faster than ours did.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.640
We didn't have enough
ingredients to make life

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.630
until we can make a planet.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:23.860
Every time a star blows up

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.920
and it has these heavy elements,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.060
it's not enough the first round.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.670
You gotta accumulate them.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.860
And we're like second or third generation

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.660
supernova here in our solar system.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.210
And only then you get
enough to have oxygen,

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:41.930
carbon, nitrogen, silicon,
iron, we got enough.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.230
We got a solid planet.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.360
- So there's more than one supernova

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.660
has to take place before enough elements--

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.440
- More than one generations
of supernova, yeah.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.130
You can add up how much that is,

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:52.770
and it's feeding the entire galaxy,

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.330
so yeah.
- Meaning a star

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.700
has to supernova, and then
a new star has to form

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:57.610
and then supernova--

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:58.530
- Correct, correct.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.560
So you need a few of those generations

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.500
to get enough density of non-original

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.160
star material to make
things like whole balls

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.274
of planets, for example.

00:06:08.274 --> 00:06:10.100
And even so, it's not very much material.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:11.500
As big as Saturn and Jupiter are,

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.640
they're made of mostly--

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:13.473
- Gas.

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:15.920
- Mostly hydrogen and
helium that were made

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:17.670
in the big bang, not made in stars.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.320
- Is it possible that
there's something else

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.130
that could be invented or created rather,

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.010
when more generations of supernovas occur

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:28.690
that hasn't happened yet?

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.490
Like it took so many
generations for planets

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.323
to--

00:06:32.323 --> 00:06:33.156
- Very interesting.

00:06:33.156 --> 00:06:35.270
So we are the product of the mixtures

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.620
of ingredients that are in this universe.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.300
So if you rank the elements in your body,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:41.910
the number one element is hydrogen,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.430
contained in the water molecule.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:44.263
'Cause we're mostly water

00:06:44.263 --> 00:06:45.930
as you may remember from biology.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:49.070
The next most common atom is oxygen.

00:06:49.070 --> 00:06:50.570
From the water molecule.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:52.053
Third most is carbon.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.470
We're carbon based life.

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:55.823
Fourth most is nitrogen.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.190
Well wait a minute, those are the top four

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.100
ingredients in the universe itself.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:02.973
In order.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:06.930
Sorry, not including helium
which is chemically inert.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.930
You couldn't use it anyway.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.660
So it's the top four chemically active

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:11.493
ingredients in the uni--

00:07:11.493 --> 00:07:12.720
we're made of that!

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.260
So you ask a brilliant question.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.590
Is there some other universe
that has a different

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:20.400
mixture of other ingredients
and they opportunistically

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.623
made stuff out of those other ingredients?

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:25.940
That's a great question.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.453
I've never thought about that.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:31.297
What would a universe be like
if it was mostly molybdenum?

00:07:31.297 --> 00:07:33.096
(chuckles) or something.

00:07:33.096 --> 00:07:34.910
Pick some elements.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.840
Pick some random other
four set of elements

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.750
and see what you can come up with.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.350
You need some chemical activity in it.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.530
Carbon is highly reactive
with other elements,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.820
so that's good.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.310
It's not an accident
that we're carbon based.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.040
We think other life
elsewhere in the universe

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.820
would be carbon based
'cause carbon is plentiful

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.100
and it bonds in so many different ways.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.290
If you experimenting with
the diversity of life,

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.828
carbon is your element to do that on.

00:08:00.828 --> 00:08:04.850
So this urge, let's see if
you have silicon based life.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.780
Sure, 'cause silicon
sits right below carbon

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:08.680
on the periodic table.

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.570
It makes all the same kinds of molecules.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:11.940
You've heard of carbon dioxide, CO2,

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.410
there's silicon dioxide, SiO2.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.550
It makes the same families of molecules.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:19.800
So you can, why not make life with,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.370
sure, maybe, but there's
five times as much carbon

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.180
in the universe than silicon.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.600
You don't have to appeal to silicon.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:28.023
Carbon is there knocking on your door.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:32.530
So for me, what's curious to me

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:35.100
is the assertion that
if we have a multiverse

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.570
and there's an infinite
number of universes,

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.190
that means there's an
infinite combinations

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.920
of all things that had
ever happened, ever,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.070
that there's another pair of us

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.600
having this conversation
in another universe

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.030
except you're wearing a red shirt

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:50.570
and I'm wearing a whatever.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.210
I mean, you make tiny changes in what is,

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:56.400
and say that's happening
in one other universe.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.880
Somewhere.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.410
And people are wondering, does
this give you immortality?

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.533
If you exist in this other universe,

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:06.493
is that really you?

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.220
If your entire molecular
structure's identical,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.730
is that really you?

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.430
And I think people are overstepping there.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:15.993
I don't think it's really you.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:20.040
It's a copy of you, but it's not you.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:24.780
And my evidence for that
is if you're a twin,

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.063
you don't wake up as your twin, ever.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.820
Even though you're molecularly identical

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:33.343
to each other from birth.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:37.750
You don't share each other's conscious.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:41.110
So one of you is an exact
replica of the other,

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:43.690
except you're not the same human beings.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:45.670
So I can make an exact
replica of you somewhere else,

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.780
but I don't think you'll
know or care or feel it.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.890
So this talk of "I will live forever,"

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.880
even when you upload your consciousness.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.920
We don't understand
consciousness enough to know

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.110
what makes you you and me me.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:00.500
Why do I wake up as me
everyday, and not as you?

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:01.823
Or as anybody else?

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.950
That's consciousness.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:08.890
Can that be carbon copied
(chuckles) carbon copied.

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:11.860
Can that be duplicated
and put somewhere else

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.460
and that still be you?

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:15.400
Or are you just making a twin?

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.733
There's a whole other
independent consciousness.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.453
That's what keeps me up at night.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:25.500
This concept of different laws of physics

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.760
is all from the quantum.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.582
- I think it's gonna
keep me up at night, too.

00:10:28.582 --> 00:10:30.140
(Neil laughs)

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:31.183
- Thanks Joe,
- Thank you.

00:10:31.183 --> 00:10:32.016
- for your question.

00:10:32.016 --> 00:10:32.960
- My pleasure.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:35.110
- This has been Star Talk.

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:38.990
A one on one with the
one and only Joe Rogan,

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:43.943
wrestling, MMAing his deepest
questions in the cosmos.

00:10:43.943 --> 00:10:45.038
(Joe laughs)

00:10:45.038 --> 00:10:47.621
(upbeat music)

